IMPORT `wk-contractors-and-claims`
IMPORT `wk-test-fixtures`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for Business Activities Affecting Workers (2025) -- BOTH SIDES of
-- every distinction the text draws, the guards (NOTHING when a finding the answer turns on
-- has not been made), the fields no rule reads (a change in one changes nothing), and one
-- fixture per fork on which the reading taken and its alternative give different answers
-- (NOTES.md, fork register, names the forks that have none). Each block cites its
-- provision and pdf page.
--
-- A failing assertion is a finding: no expected value here was edited to match the code.

§ `Boundaries, guards and forks`

§§ `Expected answers, named`

`crime` MEANS `businesses that compete with each other for workers may be committing an antitrust crime if they enter into such an agreement`
`civil` MEANS `even if criminal charges are not pursued, such an agreement may also be subject to civil liability`
`illegal even if no actual harm` MEANS `the agencies state that such an agreement, formed between competing or potentially competing employers, directly or through an intermediary, is illegal even if it did not result in actual harm such as lower wages`
`fuller analysis` MEANS `n.15: such an agreement may require a fuller analysis of its effects, the restraint being subordinate and collateral to a broader business collaboration and reasonably necessary to achieve its procompetitive potential`
`DOJ may investigate` MEANS `the DOJ may criminally investigate and, where appropriate, bring felony charges against the participants in such an agreement, including both individuals and companies`
`all four` MEANS JUST (LIST `crime`, `civil`, `illegal even if no actual harm`, `DOJ may investigate`)

`mode 1` MEANS `section 1: the agencies state that such an agreement is illegal even if it did not result in actual harm such as lower wages`
`mode n.15` MEANS `n.15: such an agreement may require a fuller analysis of its effects`
`mode 2` MEANS `section 2: the agencies state that such an agreement can be per se illegal, and may be illegal regardless of whether it actually harms workers`

`scrutiny and per se` MEANS JUST (LIST `no-poach clauses in franchise agreements are subject to antitrust scrutiny`, `the agencies state that such an agreement between a franchisor and its franchisee not to compete for workers can be per se illegal under the antitrust laws: the agreement itself may be illegal regardless of whether it actually harms workers`)
`other federal and state laws` MEANS JUST (LIST `agreements among franchisees, either written or unwritten, not to poach, hire, or solicit each other's workers may violate other federal and state laws`)

`may violate` MEANS `sharing competitively sensitive information with competitors about terms and conditions of employment may violate the antitrust laws`
`effect found` MEANS `exchanging competitively sensitive compensation or other employment information with a competitor may be unlawful when the exchange has, or is likely to have, an anticompetitive effect, whether or not that effect was intended: such an effect is found`
`effect pending` MEANS `exchanging competitively sensitive compensation or other employment information with a competitor may be unlawful when the exchange has, or is likely to have, an anticompetitive effect, whether or not that effect was intended: its effect has not been assessed`
`may indicate` MEANS `information exchanges may indicate the existence of a wage-fixing conspiracy`
`through a tool` MEANS `providing competitively sensitive information through an algorithm or through a third party's tool or product may also be unlawful`
`recommendations` MEANS `information exchanges facilitated by or through a third party, including through an algorithm or other software, that are used to generate wage or other benefit recommendations can be unlawful even if the exchange does not require businesses to strictly adhere to those recommendations`

`other federal laws` MEANS `non-competes may also violate other federal laws, such as the National Labor Relations Act and the Packers and Stockyards Act`
`state laws` MEANS `non-competes can also violate state laws, including laws banning unfair methods of competition and unfair or deceptive practices, as well as statutes banning or restricting some or all non-competes`

`same concerns` MEANS `such provisions raise many of the same antitrust concerns as non-competes`
`state limits` MEANS `state law also sets limits on training repayment requirements and NDA restrictions`
`may also investigate` MEANS `the agencies may also investigate and take action against other restrictive agreements that impede worker mobility or otherwise undermine competition`

`the Overview's contractor sentence` MEANS `the Overview: an agreement between businesses to fix the compensation that each pays to independent contractors may violate the antitrust laws, just as an agreement between businesses to fix the wages each pays to workers may violate the antitrust laws`
`the platforms sentence` MEANS `an agreement between two or more competing platforms to fix the compensation of independent contractors offering their services via the platforms may constitute the type of per se violation of the antitrust laws that exposes the platforms to criminal liability`

`may investigate a claim` MEANS `the agencies may investigate and take action against businesses that make false or misleading claims about potential earnings that workers, including both employees and independent contractors, may realize`

§§ `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers [pdf page 4; 1, pdf page 6]`

#ASSERT `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` `potentially competing employers` EQUALS JUST `the businesses are potentially competing employers, not shown to compete in the same labor market`
-- The guard: Quiet names no labor market, and there is no finding of potential competition.
#ASSERT `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` `businesses whose labor markets are not stated` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (`the pair` `Quiet` `Quiet` FALSE FALSE) EQUALS NOTHING
-- The finding of potential competition answers even where the labor markets are not named.
#ASSERT `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (`the pair` `Acme` `Quiet` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS JUST `the businesses are potentially competing employers, not shown to compete in the same labor market`
-- A shared labor market wins over the finding of potential competition.
#ASSERT `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (`the pair` `Acme` `Bolt` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS JUST `the businesses compete for workers in the same labor market`
-- One shared market among several is enough (fork F1).
#ASSERT `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (`the pair` (`a business named` "Dune" (LIST "nurses", "drivers") EMPTY) (`a business named` "Elm" (LIST "cooks", "drivers") EMPTY) FALSE FALSE) EQUALS JUST `the businesses compete for workers in the same labor market`
-- Neither the products nor the collaborative relationship is read.
#ASSERT `General principles: whether the businesses compete for workers` (`the pair` `Acme` `Cove` FALSE TRUE) EQUALS JUST `the businesses do not compete for workers in any labor market named`

§§ `General principles: the evidence of an agreement [pdf page 4]`

`no evidence` MEANS `Evidence of an agreement` WITH
    `a business has discussed with another company wages or other potential terms of employment` IS FALSE
    `a business has engaged in parallel behavior that demonstrates a shared understanding` IS FALSE
    `a business has invited another company to participate in a plan to restrict competition for workers, followed by action consistent with that plan` IS FALSE
    `a business has used a common intermediary to obtain competitively sensitive information` IS FALSE
    `other direct or circumstantial evidence offered` IS EMPTY
`parallel behavior only` MEANS `Evidence of an agreement` WITH
    `a business has discussed with another company wages or other potential terms of employment` IS FALSE
    `a business has engaged in parallel behavior that demonstrates a shared understanding` IS TRUE
    `a business has invited another company to participate in a plan to restrict competition for workers, followed by action consistent with that plan` IS FALSE
    `a business has used a common intermediary to obtain competitively sensitive information` IS FALSE
    `other direct or circumstantial evidence offered` IS EMPTY
`other evidence only` MEANS `Evidence of an agreement` WITH
    `a business has discussed with another company wages or other potential terms of employment` IS FALSE
    `a business has engaged in parallel behavior that demonstrates a shared understanding` IS FALSE
    `a business has invited another company to participate in a plan to restrict competition for workers, followed by action consistent with that plan` IS FALSE
    `a business has used a common intermediary to obtain competitively sensitive information` IS FALSE
    `other direct or circumstantial evidence offered` IS LIST "a shared spreadsheet of pay bands", "a trade association's minutes"
#ASSERT `General principles: the kinds of evidence of an agreement that are present` `no evidence` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `General principles: the kinds of evidence of an agreement that are present` `parallel behavior only` EQUALS LIST `parallel behavior that demonstrates a shared understanding`
-- "For example": evidence of another kind is reported, once (fork F3).
#ASSERT `General principles: the kinds of evidence of an agreement that are present` `other evidence only` EQUALS LIST `other direct or circumstantial evidence`

§§ `General principles: whether the agreement's effects matter [pdf page 4]`

`effects matter` MEANS `General principles: what the text says of whether the agreement's effects matter`
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an identified agreement` `potentially competing employers` `wage fixing at a level`) EQUALS JUST (LIST `mode 1`)
-- n.15 needs both conjuncts: either alone leaves section 1's sentence (fork F5).
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an identified agreement with the n.15 findings` `competitors for workers` `no-hire` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS JUST (LIST `mode 1`)
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an identified agreement with the n.15 findings` `competitors for workers` `no-hire` FALSE TRUE) EQUALS JUST (LIST `mode 1`)
-- Section 2's franchisor and franchisee, not competing for workers: section 2 alone (fork F7).
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an identified franchise agreement` `businesses that do not compete for workers` `no-hire` `a franchisor and its franchisee` FALSE) EQUALS JUST (LIST `mode 2`)
-- ... and competing for workers: both sections' statements, with no precedence (fork F20).
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an identified franchise agreement` `competitors for workers` `no-hire` `a franchisor and its franchisee` FALSE) EQUALS JUST (LIST `mode 1`, `mode 2`)
-- n.15 replaces section 1's sentence only, not section 2's (fork F5).
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`the agreement in full` `the agencies identify an agreement between the businesses` `competitors for workers` `no-hire` FALSE `employees` FALSE `a franchisor and its franchisee` FALSE TRUE TRUE `a formal written agreement`) EQUALS JUST (LIST `mode n.15`, `mode 2`)
-- The text does not say which way another type goes ("Some types", "including"): NOTHING (fork F3).
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an identified agreement` `competitors for workers` `something else relating to workers`) EQUALS NOTHING
-- The starting point "may be" wage fixing: no mode is stated for it (fork F6).
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an identified agreement` `competitors for workers` `to set a starting point for compensation`) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an identified agreement` `businesses that do not compete for workers` `wage fixing at a level`) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Guards: "If the Agencies identify an agreement".
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an agreement with the finding` `the agencies find no agreement between the businesses` `competitors for workers` `wage fixing at a level`) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an agreement with the finding` `whether there is an agreement has not been determined` `competitors for workers` `wage fixing at a level`) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `effects matter` (`an identified agreement` `businesses whose labor markets are not stated` `wage fixing at a level`) EQUALS NOTHING

§§ `Section 1: the substance [pdf pages 5-6]`

#ASSERT `1: what the agreement is, in substance` `something else relating to workers` EQUALS NOTHING

§§ `Section 1: what it says [pdf pages 5-6]`

`section 1` MEANS `1: what section 1 says of the agreement`
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `competitors for workers` `no-hire`) EQUALS `all four`
-- The cold-call field is not read: allowed or not, the same.
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement not to cold call workers` `competitors for workers` FALSE) EQUALS `all four`
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `competitors for workers` `to request permission from the other company before trying to hire an employee`) EQUALS `all four`
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `competitors for workers` `to restrict a company's ability to hire another company's workers, without completely prohibiting it`) EQUALS `all four`
-- Potentially competing employers: not the crime or civil sentences (fork F4).
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `potentially competing employers` `wage fixing at a level`) EQUALS JUST (LIST `illegal even if no actual harm`, `DOJ may investigate`)
-- The starting point, between potentially competing employers: its one sentence (fork F6);
-- between businesses that do not compete for workers: none.
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `potentially competing employers` `to set a starting point for compensation`) EQUALS JUST (LIST `an agreement to set a starting point for compensation may be a form of wage fixing under the law`)
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `businesses that do not compete for workers` `to set a starting point for compensation`) EQUALS JUST EMPTY
-- n.15: each conjunct alone leaves section 1's sentence; both replace it, for potential
-- competitors too (fork F5).
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement with the n.15 findings` `competitors for workers` `no-hire` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS `all four`
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement with the n.15 findings` `competitors for workers` `no-hire` FALSE TRUE) EQUALS `all four`
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement with the n.15 findings` `potentially competing employers` `wage fixing at a level` TRUE TRUE) EQUALS JUST (LIST `fuller analysis`, `DOJ may investigate`)
-- The general principles' collaborative relationship is not n.15's finding (fork F5).
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` (`the pair` `Acme` `Bolt` FALSE TRUE) `no-hire`) EQUALS `all four`
-- Businesses that do not compete for workers: no sentence of section 1.
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `businesses that do not compete for workers` `no-hire`) EQUALS JUST EMPTY
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `competitors for workers` `something else relating to workers`) EQUALS JUST EMPTY
-- Guards and the three findings on an agreement.
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an agreement with the finding` `the agencies find no agreement between the businesses` `competitors for workers` `no-hire`) EQUALS JUST EMPTY
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an agreement with the finding` `whether there is an agreement has not been determined` `competitors for workers` `no-hire`) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `businesses whose labor markets are not stated` `no-hire`) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `businesses whose labor markets are not stated` `to set a starting point for compensation`) EQUALS NOTHING
-- ... but an agreement none of section 1's sentences is about does not turn on it.
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement` `businesses whose labor markets are not stated` `something else relating to workers`) EQUALS JUST EMPTY
-- The kind of workers is not read (6: workers include independent contractors; fork F10).
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement about` `competitors for workers` `wage fixing at a level` `independent contractors` FALSE) EQUALS `all four`
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified agreement about` `competitors for workers` `no-hire` `both employees and independent contractors` FALSE) EQUALS `all four`
-- A franchise relationship does not take an agreement out of section 1 ("also subject").
#ASSERT `section 1` (`an identified franchise agreement` `competitors for workers` `no-hire` `a franchisor and its franchisee` FALSE) EQUALS `all four`

